---
title: Two-factor authentication
description: Optional, off until you turn it on, and the one thing that stops your email inbox from being enough to sign in as you.
---

Two-factor authentication adds a six-digit code after either sign-in method. It is optional and off
until you turn it on, from the **Security** panel in [Settings](/account/settings/).

It matters more here than in a product with passwords. Baseout stores no password, so a magic link
in your inbox is the whole credential. Two-factor is what makes reading your email insufficient.

In this guide, you will:

- Enroll an authenticator app, and save the backup codes it hands you
- Sign in with a code, a backup code, or a device you trusted
- Get back in without the app, and turn two-factor off again

## Turning it on

Enrollment runs in three steps.

:::note
Two-factor is not active until the third step passes, so leaving enrollment halfway leaves the
account exactly as it was.
:::

{% steps %}

1. **Scan the code** with an authenticator app, or enter the secret by hand if you cannot scan.
2. **Type a code back** to verify the app and Baseout agree.
3. **Save your backup codes.** They are shown once, at this moment, with copy and download, and you
   confirm you saved them before the screen closes.

{% /steps %}

### About the backup codes

They are the way back in when you do not have the app. Keep them somewhere that is not the phone
holding the authenticator, since losing the phone is the case they exist for.

:::note
Regenerating them later invalidates the whole old set. Do that if a copy may have been seen, and
remember to replace any copy you had stored elsewhere.
:::

## Signing in afterwards

Both sign-in methods gain one step: a six-digit code, in an input that advances box by box and
accepts a paste of the whole code.

**Use a backup code** swaps the input for a backup code.
**Trust this device for 30 days** skips the step on that browser for that long. See
[Sessions and devices](/account/sessions/).

A wrong code says so in plain words. Repeated wrong codes show you the wait rather than a dead end.

## If you lost your authenticator

A backup code gets you in, which is what they are for. Use one, then re-enroll with a new app and
save the new codes.

If you have neither the app nor a code, [contact support](/contact/) from the address on the
account. We can see whether an account exists for an address without you being signed in, which is
where that conversation starts.

## Turning it off

Turning two-factor off asks for a current code first, so it cannot be switched off from a session
somebody left open. That is the point of asking, and it is why keeping a backup code somewhere
reachable is worth the small effort.

